<body>
<div class="ltx_page_main">
<div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Invariants of a general branched cover of <math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Gabriel Bujokas &amp; Anand Patel
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Department of Mathematics, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:gbujokas@math.harvard.edu">gbujokas@math.harvard.edu</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Department of Mathematics, Boston College, Chestnut Hill, MA 02467
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:anand.patel@bc.edu">anand.patel@bc.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Date: August 8, 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate the resolution of a <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">general</em> branched cover <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in its relative canonical embedding <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math>. We conjecture that the syzygy bundles appearing in the resolution are balanced for a general cover, provided that the genus is sufficiently large compared to the degree. We prove this for the <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Casnati-Ekedahl</em> bundle, or <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bundle of quadrics</em> <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>—the first bundle appearing in the resolution of the ideal of the relative canonical embedding. Furthermore, we prove the conjecture for all syzygy bundles in the resolution when the genus satisfies <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1\mod d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>mod</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1\mod d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every degree <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> branched cover <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> from a genus <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curve <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has a natural <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative canonical embedding</em></p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota\colon C\hookrightarrow{\bf P}E" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota\colon C\hookrightarrow{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">into a projective bundle <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is the classical <em id="S1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Tschirnhausen bundle</em> - the dual of the sheaf of traceless functions on <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, viewed as an <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module. Geometrically, the scroll <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math> is swept out by the spans</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\alpha^{-1}(t)\rangle\subset{\bf P}^{g-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\alpha^{-1}(t)\rangle\subset{\bf P}^{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The embedding <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> is such that the projection <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon{\bf P}E\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon{\bf P}E\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> induces the map <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, as illustrated in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span id="S1.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:218.98pt;height:246.44284676840002pt;"></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>A cover sitting in its relative canonical scroll.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The ideal sheaf <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{I}}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{I}}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math> has a well known relative minimal resolution, due to a theorem of Schreyer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch86</a>]</cite>, generalized later by Casnati and Ekedahl <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">CE96</a>]</cite>. This resolution of <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math> involves <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">syzygy bundles</em> <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,...,d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,...,d-2</annotation></semantics></math>, which are vector bundles on the target <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The splitting types of <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are then fundamental algebro-geometric invariants naturally attached to any branched cover <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since the set of all branched covers of fixed degree and genus forms the irreducible <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hurwitz space</em> <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that the syzygy bundles <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> have a generic isomorphism type for a Zariski open subset of covers. Our general objective is to understand the generic splitting types of the syzygy bundles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It is reasonable to expect that the bundles <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">balanced</em> for a general cover, in the sense that the largest degree of a summand of <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is at most one more than the smallest degree. This is succinctly written as</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(\End N_{i})=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(\End N_{i})=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Being balanced is certainly an open condition, in the sense that in a “typical” family of vector bundles on <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, one expects an open set of bundles to be balanced. With this expectation in mind, and with the aid of explicit examples in low genera and degree, we make the following conjecture:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The general cover <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> has balanced syzygy bundles when <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\gg d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≫</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\gg d</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before considering the syzygy bundles <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, one might ask about the generic behavior of the Tschirnhausen bundle <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. A theorem of Coppens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cop99</a>]</cite> and Ballico <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bal89</a>]</cite> shows that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is balanced for a generic cover.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>bears a striking resemblence to Green’s conjecture on syzygies of canonical curves. It seems difficult to precisely identify the precise relationship between the two conjectures. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bop14a</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This paper focuses specifically on the first bundle <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we refer to simply as <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is known as the <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bundle of quadrics</em> or the <em id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Casnati-Ekedahl bundle</em> - for simplicity we will often call it the <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle. The geometric interpretation of <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is very simple: <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizes the vector space of quadrics containing the <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points of <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math> fibers of <math id="S1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In recent work of Bopp and Hahn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bop14b</a>]</cite>, the precise necessary relationship between <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is studied within the regime where the Brill-Noether number satisfies <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(g,d,1)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(g,d,1)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;d+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;d+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In this <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,d)</annotation></semantics></math>-regime, they conclude that the generic bundle of quadrics <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is balanced if and only if <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-\rho-\frac{7}{2})^{2}-2d+\frac{23}{4}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>23</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-\rho-\frac{7}{2})^{2}-2d+\frac{23}{4}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, with the use of their <em id="S1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Macaulay2</em>-Package <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH15</a>]</cite>, their experimentation leads them to make the following refinement of <a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Bopp, Hahn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bop14b</a>]</cite>)</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> then the bundle </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is balanced for a general cover </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and if we write </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}=\oplus\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}^{1}}(a^{(i)}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}=\oplus\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}^{1}}(a^{(i)}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then the following bound holds for a general </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{j,l}|a^{(i)}_{j}-a^{(i)}_{l}|\leq\min\{g-d-1,i+2\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{j,l}|a^{(i)}_{j}-a^{(i)}_{l}|\leq\min\{g-d-1,i+2\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that the <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=d+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=d+1</annotation></semantics></math> case of part <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2)</annotation></semantics></math> is an instance of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="2.4. ‣ 2.2. Evidence ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Our main result is the proof of the <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> case of <a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmaintheoremx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_maintheorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmaintheoremx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main Theorem</span></span><span id="Thmmaintheoremx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmaintheoremx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmaintheoremx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmaintheoremx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmmaintheoremx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq(d-3)(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq(d-3)(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the bundle of quadrics <math id="Thmmaintheoremx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of a general cover <math id="Thmmaintheoremx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is balanced.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The proof proceeds via degeneration. We begin in the first section by reviewing the Casnati-Ekedahl structure theorem, and then studying the bundle of quadrics for covers of genus <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. We then glue such covers together in a “chain” to create an admissible cover <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon X\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon X\to P</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a chain of <math id="S1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Once we have constructed this cover, we must argue that its <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle, a vector bundle on the chain of rational curves <math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, is balanced.
In order to do this, we first provide a general criterion for a vector bundle <math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> on a chain <math id="S1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> to satisfy <math id="S1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(\End V)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(\End V)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
<a href="#S3" title="3. Vector bundles on rational chains ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is devoted to establishing this criterion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">We are then left with the task of verifying that these conditions are met for the <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle of <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon X\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon X\to P</annotation></semantics></math>. This, in turn, leads us to considering a degree <math id="S1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> maximal rank problem for “maximally connected chains” of rational normal curves in projective space. <a href="#S4" title="4. Balanced 
        
          F
        
      -bundles and a maximal rank problem ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is devoted to establishing the connection between our original problem of showing <math id="S1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(\End F)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(\End F)=0</annotation></semantics></math> with the degree <math id="S1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> maximal rank problem.
We settle the aforementioned maximal rank problem in <a href="#S5" title="5. The maximal rank problem for maximally connected chains ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout, we will work over an algebraically closed field <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of characteristic zero.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Acknowledgments</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to thank Christian Bopp for interesting conversations and for sharing his joint work with Michael Hahn. We thank Eric Larson for suggesting <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="5.3. ‣ Proof of . ‣ 5. The maximal rank problem for maximally connected chains ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>. We also had several conversations with Anand Deopurkar, Joe Harris and Eric Riedl which were very helpful. We also thank Carolina Yamate for her crucial assistance in producing the pictures for this paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We review the basic Casnati-Ekedahl structure theorem of branched covers of algebraic varieties. We then examine the Casnati-Ekedahl resolution of covers <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> when the genus <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(C)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>The Casnati-Ekedahl Resolution</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be integral schemes and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> a finite flat Gorenstein morphism of degree <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. The map <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> gives an exact sequence</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{O}_{Y}\to\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X}\to{E_{\alpha}}^{\vee}\to 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∨</mo></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{O}_{Y}\to\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X}\to{E_{\alpha}}^{\vee}\to 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector bundle of rank <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, called the <em id="S2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Tschirnhausen bundle</em> of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> the dualizing sheaf of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom_{Y}(-,\mathcal{O}_{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom_{Y}(-,\mathcal{O}_{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> to (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1. The Casnati-Ekedahl Resolution ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), we get</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to E\to\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}\to\mathcal{O}_{Y}\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to E\to\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}\to\mathcal{O}_{Y}\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a map <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}E\to\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}E\to\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">CE96</a><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Theorem 2.1]</span></cite><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
In the above setup, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}E\to\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}E\to\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> gives an embedding <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota\colon X\to{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota\colon X\to{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\pi\circ\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\pi\circ\iota</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon{\bf P}E\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon{\bf P}E\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection. Moreover, the subscheme <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math> can be described as follows.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The resolution of </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as an </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}E}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-module has the form</span></p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}0\to\pi^{*}N_{d-2}(-d)\to\pi^{*}N_{d-3}(-d+2)\to\pi^{*}N_{d-4}(-d+3)\to\dots{\\
}\dots\to\pi^{*}N_{2}(-3)\to\pi^{*}N_{1}(-2)\to\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}E}\to\mathcal{O}_{X}\to 0,\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mi>
</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}0\to\pi^{*}N_{d-2}(-d)\to\pi^{*}N_{d-3}(-d+2)\to\pi^{*}N_{d-4}(-d+3)\to\dots{\\
}\dots\to\pi^{*}N_{2}(-3)\to\pi^{*}N_{1}(-2)\to\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}E}\to\mathcal{O}_{X}\to 0,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are vector bundles on </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Restricted to a point </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, this sequence is the minimal free resolution of length </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> zero dimensional scheme </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{y}\subset{\bf P}E_{y}\cong{\bf P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{y}\subset{\bf P}E_{y}\cong{\bf P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The ranks of the </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are given by</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rk N_{i}=\frac{i(d-2-i)}{d-1}\binom{d}{i+1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rk N_{i}=\frac{i(d-2-i)}{d-1}\binom{d}{i+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d-2}\cong\det E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≅</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d-2}\cong\det E</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(d)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, the resolution is symmetric, that is, isomorphic to the resolution obtained by applying the functor
</span><math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom_{\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}E}}(-,\pi^{*}N_{d-2}(-d))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom_{\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}E}}(-,\pi^{*}N_{d-2}(-d))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will only use the case <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y={\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y={\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which was discovered by Schreyer in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch86</a>, Corollary 4.4]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The branch divisor of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is given by a section of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\det E)^{\otimes 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\det E)^{\otimes 2}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, if <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a curve of (arithmetic) genus <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y={\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y={\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rk E=d-1\text{ and }\deg E=g+d-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rk E=d-1\text{ and }\deg E=g+d-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will be concerned with the vector bundle <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which from here onwards we denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, and refer to as the <em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bundle of quadrics</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that, by twisting the Casnati-Ekedahl resolution (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ Item (a) ‣ 2.1. ‣ 2.1. The Casnati-Ekedahl Resolution ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) by <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}E}(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}E}(2)</annotation></semantics></math> and then applying <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain an exact sequence</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to F\to\Sym^{2}E\to\alpha_{*}(\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes 2})\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to F\to\Sym^{2}E\to\alpha_{*}(\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes 2})\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">From this sequence, it is easy to see that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rk F=d(d-3)/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rk F=d(d-3)/2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg F=(d-3)(g+d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg F=(d-3)(g+d-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="2.1. ‣ 2.1. The Casnati-Ekedahl Resolution ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> allows us to associate to every cover <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> a collection of vector bundles <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E,F,...,N_{d-2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E,F,...,N_{d-2})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. <a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> expresses the natural expectation that these vector bundles are all <em id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">balanced</em> for a general cover <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (we say a vector bundle <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is balanced if <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(\End V)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(\End V)=0</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To fix notation, we let <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> denote the divisor class associated to the line bundle <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}E}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}E}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> on a projective bundle <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math> to be the scheme <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Proj(\Sym^{*}E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Proj</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Proj(\Sym^{*}E)</annotation></semantics></math>, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har77</a>]</cite>. We let <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> denote the class of a fiber of the projection <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon{\bf P}E\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon{\bf P}E\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Evidence</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We explain the rationale behind <a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, when the degree is at most <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, it is known that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is generically balanced: <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP15</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bop14b</a>]</cite>. In these cases, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is (up to twist) the only bundle appearing in the Casnati-Ekedahl resolution, due to the symmetry of the resolution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=4</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle is generically <em id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> balanced:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a general degree <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> cover, with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> a genus <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>,
non-hyperelliptic curve. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=\mathcal{O}(1)\oplus\mathcal{O}(2)^{\oplus 4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\mathcal{O}(1)\oplus\mathcal{O}(2)^{\oplus 4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a sub vector bundle of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sym^{2}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sym^{2}E</annotation></semantics></math>, we conclude that the degree of any summand of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> cannot exceed <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, since <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg F=27" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg F=27</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rk F=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rk F=9</annotation></semantics></math>, the bundle <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is imbalanced if and only if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(4)</annotation></semantics></math> is a summand of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We show that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(F(-4))\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(F(-4))\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> by considering the sequence (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2.1. The Casnati-Ekedahl Resolution ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). We want
to show that the map <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},\Sym^{2}E(-4))\to H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},\alpha_{*}(\omega_{\alpha})(-4))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},\Sym^{2}E(-4))\to H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},\alpha_{*}(\omega_{\alpha})(-4))</annotation></semantics></math> is not injective. But we can identify this map with:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}({\bf P}E,2\zeta-4f)\to H^{0}(C,(2\zeta-4f)|_{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}({\bf P}E,2\zeta-4f)\to H^{0}(C,(2\zeta-4f)|_{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The linear system <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\zeta-2f|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\zeta-2f|</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to the full canonical series on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, the sections of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\zeta-4f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\zeta-4f</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained by taking sums of products of sections of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\zeta-2f|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\zeta-2f|</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the canonical model of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> lies on a unique quadric in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that there is a unique element of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|2\zeta-4f|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|2\zeta-4f|</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that the map (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.3. ‣ 2.2. Evidence ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) is not injective, which implies that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> contains an <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(4)</annotation></semantics></math> summand. Therefore <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is not balanced.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The above example suggests that when the genus is small compared to the degree, the <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle may be imbalanced. This example can be generalized as long as <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is somewhat small compared to <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and it explains the inequality <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\gg d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≫</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\gg d</annotation></semantics></math> in
<a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Substantial support for the conjecture is provided by the following proposition:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="A. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is true whenever <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1\mod d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>mod</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1\mod d</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, for a general cover <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the syzygy bundles <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are <em id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">perfectly</em> balanced, i.e.</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}=\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}^{1}}(k_{i})^{\oplus r_{i}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}=\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}^{1}}(k_{i})^{\oplus r_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for some integers <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i},r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i},r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We’ll need the following basic lemma about elliptic normal curves of degree <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subset{\bf P}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subset{\bf P}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> smooth elliptic normal curve, let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a hyperplane in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z:=H\cap E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z:=H\cap E</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If</span></p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to F_{1}\to...\to F_{k}\to{{\mathcal{I}}}_{E\subset{\bf P}^{d-1}}\to 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to F_{1}\to...\to F_{k}\to{{\mathcal{I}}}_{E\subset{\bf P}^{d-1}}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is the minimal free resolution of </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subset{\bf P}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subset{\bf P}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to F_{1}|_{H}\to...\to F_{k}|_{H}\to{{\mathcal{I}}}_{Z\subset H}\to 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to F_{1}|_{H}\to...\to F_{k}|_{H}\to{{\mathcal{I}}}_{Z\subset H}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is the minimal free resolution of </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The syzygy bundles </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> appearing in the minimal free resolution (</span><a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Item (a) ‣ 2.5. ‣ 2.2. Evidence ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Invariants of a general branched cover of 
      
        
          
          P
          1" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">) are of the form </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}^{d-1}}(-m_{i})^{\oplus r_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{{\bf P}^{d-1}}(-m_{i})^{\oplus r_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for some integers </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, i.e. all summands of </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> have the same degree.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">There is an exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to{\mathcal{I}}_{E\subset{\bf P}^{d-1}}(-H)\to{\mathcal{I}}_{E\subset{\bf P}^{d-1}}\to{\mathcal{I}}_{Z\subset H}\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to{\mathcal{I}}_{E\subset{\bf P}^{d-1}}(-H)\to{\mathcal{I}}_{E\subset{\bf P}^{d-1}}\to{\mathcal{I}}_{Z\subset H}\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">Take the minimal resolution of <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{I}}_{E\subset{\bf P}^{d-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{I}}_{E\subset{\bf P}^{d-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to F_{1}\to...\to F_{k}\to{\mathcal{I}}_{E\subset{\bf P}^{d-1}}\to 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to F_{1}\to...\to F_{k}\to{\mathcal{I}}_{E\subset{\bf P}^{d-1}}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">And form the following diagram</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
